 "grid_note": "HOW THE CELLS WERE LOCATED, because a mark one column off is a person of the wrong age and the method has to be inspectable. The 39 vertical rules were fitted as a single pitch off the PRINTED HEADING band of the same image — where the boxed column labels rule strongly enough to be found — by least squares over every rule the band gives up, and the fit was checked against the heading itself: column 1 must read `Under 5` and column 27 `Under 10` under FREE COLORED MALES before a single cell is read. The sheet is scanned with a LEAN, and the lean is measured rather than assumed: the same 39-rule model is re-fitted every 250 px down the page and the drift read off, which on this deposit runs between -30 px and +18 px from heading to footing — up to half a column, and it has the opposite SIGN on different images of the same book. A grid carried over from a neighbouring page is therefore wrong by half a column at the foot, which is exactly how a mark lands in the next age band; T-0534's first reading of printed 231 was made on such a carried-over grid and its bottom third had to be read again. The 30 or 31 ruled lines were fitted the same way and checked against the committed names, block by block, with the name column rendered beside the cells. Every cell was then read as an image at the fitted grid, with the column number drawn on the crop. No cell value here is an OCR output.",
 "cells_state": "partial",
 "cells_note": "A COLUMN IS COMMITTED ONLY WHERE IT BALANCES: the lines this pass read must SUM to the figure the enumerator wrote at the foot of his own sheet, and a column that does not is left unreconciled with its residual stated rather than forced, because a half-checked column is worse than an unread one. `cells` carries only the balanced columns. `cells_first_pass` carries the whole reading, balanced or not, so the next reader starts from a reading rather than from the sheet — it is NOT reconciled data and nothing downstream may treat it as such. A blank cell on the schedule IS a zero and is recorded by absence; no line was skipped. 36 of 38 columns balance the enumerator's own footings on a THIRTY-line reading, including the two heaviest on the page — column 5 at 31 and column 18 at 24. Column 5's balance settles a glyph the reading turned on: line 3 carries an EIGHT there, not a three, and the footing only closes with the eight in it. The two columns that do not balance are named rather than smoothed. Column 1 (free white males Under 5) reads 9 against a footing this pass reads as 14: six lines — 16, 19, 22, 28, 29 and 30 — carry ink inside column 1 that is the terminal FLOURISH of the name written beside it, and this pass judged all six to be flourish rather than stroke. If the footing is 14 then five of those six are strokes, and those six cells are exactly where the next reader should start; the figure itself was read at `low` because its second glyph is angular where the same hand's 9 — column 14's footing, 19 — is looped. Column 6 (free white males 30 under 40) reads 8 against a footing of 7, and that 7 is unambiguous at magnification (a barred top and a long diagonal), so one of this pass's eight strokes in that column is not the enumerator's: lines 2, 3, 4, 8, 15, 18, 20 and 28 are the eight it found.",
